    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d626">

                <pb facs="0609" n="519" sameAs="frus:frus1918Russiav02/pg519"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/2648</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Archangel</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-09-04T21:00:00+04:31"><hi rendition="#italic">September 4,
                                1918, 9 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received September 7, 7.36
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>381. Lindley says 3,000 tons flour will arrive from England 10th September and
                    6,000 tons additional about the 15th. I hoped we would be first to report food
                    shipments.</p>
                <p>About 4,800 American troops on three transports arrived here to-day, Colonel
                    Stewart commanding.</p>
                <p>Naval Lieutenant Riis also arrived on transport not touching Murman where he had
                    orders to report from Admiral Strauss but no official designated in order. He
                    brought two boiler makers with orders to report to <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Olympia</hi>. Riis speaks Russian and Esthonian. I have designated him
                    acting naval attaché until otherwise instructed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
